    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Industrial recycling returns recovered materials to production, but collection alone does not establish successful recovery. Steel provides a useful introduction to the importance of scrap origin, sorting, and material quality when examining Iran’s recycling industries.",
    "tags": ["Industrial Recycling", "Steel Scrap", "Materials Recovery", "Circular Economy"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:30:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:30:46Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Industrial Recycling in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An evidence-limited introduction to Iranian industrial recycling, explaining steel scrap, sorting, contamination, and unresolved national data gaps."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "iran-research-context",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An industrial input, with an incomplete national picture",
        "content": "Industrial recycling is best understood as the preparation of recovered material for another productive use. For Iran, a published study of the steel-scrap industry identifies obstacles to recycling development and examines strategies for addressing them. The accessible abstract establishes that scrap supply and industrial organization were research concerns; it does not establish the condition of the sector at the present editorial cutoff. The full article was not accessible during this research. [s1]\n\nThis distinction matters because an older diagnosis cannot verify current processing capacity, collection performance, or the success of later investments. The technical explanation below therefore separates generally documented steel-recycling processes from claims about Iranian implementation.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "scrap-origins",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different scrap streams require different handling",
        "content": "Steel scrap has several origins. Internal scrap arises within steelmaking and rolling operations. External supplies include offcuts from manufacturing and material recovered from products after use. Jernkontoret, the Swedish steel producers’ association, explains that these materials differ in dimensions, chemical composition, and origin; sorting them into suitable classes supports effective remelting and trade. Its descriptions concern steel-industry practice and Swedish classification arrangements, not an Iranian regulatory standard. [s2]\n\nThe distinction helps frame an Iranian evidence search. A factory’s return of its own offcuts to production is a different activity from collecting and dismantling obsolete machinery. Recording both under a single recycling total would conceal differences in collection requirements and material quality. A credible national account needs to identify the stream being measured before comparing processors or years.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "quality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why recovered tonnes are not the whole story",
        "content": "Worldsteel’s technical fact sheet explains that scrap is an input to steelmaking, while the amount becoming available depends partly on when existing products reach the end of their useful lives. Recoverability therefore does not mean that enough suitable scrap is immediately available to replace all primary inputs. These are global technical considerations, not measured Iranian scrap balances. [s3]\n\nQuality also matters. Copper contamination can damage steel properties and cannot simply be removed during conventional steelmaking. Sorting and dilution are ways of managing it. Better separation before remelting can therefore matter as much as the quantity collected. A claim that material has been gathered is not, by itself, evidence that it meets the requirements of a particular steel product. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-needed",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains to be established in Iran",
        "content": "The available evidence supports a technical introduction, but not a comprehensive account of Iran’s industrial recycling sector. Steel cannot stand in for every material, and descriptions of international practice cannot verify local environmental performance. Publication of a fuller Iranian account requires current evidence on processors, material streams, recovery yields, residual disposal, and regional differences.\n\nThe useful starting question is consequently precise: which material, recovered from which source, becomes which usable input? Answering it would make recycling claims comparable and distinguish industrial recovery from collection statistics alone.",
        "source_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
