  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-17-6-5",
    "page_path": "/explore/mining/industrial-development/production-efficiency/",
    "name": "Production Efficiency",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/mining/industrial-development/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Production efficiency concerns the relationship between industrial inputs and useful output. Iran’s industrial energy-management initiatives provide a documented example, while also showing why training, certification, and measured savings must be assessed separately.",
    "tags": ["Production Efficiency", "Energy Management", "Industrial Performance", "Iranian Industry"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:30:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:30:46Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Production Efficiency in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore industrial efficiency in Iran through energy management, performance measurement, and the distinction between certification and verified savings."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "measurement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Efficiency begins with a defined comparison",
        "content": "Production efficiency asks how inputs relate to useful output. Energy performance is one important part of that question, but a country’s energy intensity is not a direct reading of factory efficiency. The International Energy Agency explains that energy use relative to economic output also reflects economic structure: an economy concentrated in heavy industry differs from one concentrated in services. Iran’s national intensity therefore needs interpretation before it is used to judge individual plants. [s1]\n\nThe same caution applies to change over time. A national indicator can describe the overall relationship between energy and economic activity without identifying the contribution of a particular industrial improvement. For a factory-level claim, the evidence needs to match the process, output, and period being discussed.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-programme",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented Iranian energy-efficiency initiative",
        "content": "UNIDO’s independent terminal evaluation documents the project Industrial Energy Efficiency in Key Sectors in Iran. UNIDO is the United Nations Industrial Development Organization; the project received financing from the Global Environment Facility and worked with the Iranian Fuel Conservation Company as an executing partner. Its targeted sectors were iron and steel, petrochemicals, refineries, brick, and cement. [s2]\n\nThe project combined policy-related work, sharing of good practices, training and benchmarking, direct industrial support, and financial support. This design treated efficiency as a matter of organizational capability and incentives as well as equipment. The evaluation is historical evidence of a particular intervention; it does not establish the performance of every Iranian plant or verify continuing results through the editorial cutoff. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "management-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What an energy-management system contributes",
        "content": "ISO 50001 provides a framework for an energy-management system: a continuing organizational process for understanding and improving energy performance. ISO describes the use of energy-performance indicators and baselines to track improvement. The framework follows a cycle of planning, implementation, checking, and improvement, and it does not prescribe a particular energy-saving technology. [s3]\n\nThat approach makes measurement part of routine management. An indicator can be a simple ratio or a more complex model, depending on the organization’s needs. The relevant question is whether the selected measure represents the operation being assessed and supports a meaningful comparison. The existence of a management framework and the size of a measured improvement are consequently separate pieces of evidence. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "certification-and-results",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Certification is not a national savings series",
        "content": "ISO explicitly states that certification is possible but not obligatory: an organization may implement an energy-management system without obtaining a certificate. Certification counts therefore do not enumerate all organizations using such practices. They also do not provide the underlying energy observations needed to calculate national industrial savings. [s4]\n\nFor Iran, the next editorial step is to connect documented initiatives with comparable operating evidence. This draft establishes the purpose of energy management and a historical Iranian programme, but it cannot rank sectors or report a national efficiency gain. A stronger account would include measured results, their baselines, and the limits of comparison, alongside evidence on material losses and other production inputs.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
